EXTENDING THE NED LIVE STATUS CAPABILITY USING CUSTOM TEXTFSM PARSER TEMPLATES
==============================================================================

1. Introduction
----------------
   This instruction shows how the live status API in this NED can easily be
   extended with custom templates for reading additional operational data
   from a device.

   The TextFSM template format has been selected for this feature. The reason
   for this is that TextFSM is a de facto standard among network engineers
   and fairly easy to use.

2. Getting started
------------------
   The TextFSM parser is a tool developed by Google and solely written in
   Python. This tool together with a a complete Python environment must be
   installed on the host running NSO.

   1. Install Python 2.7 or later:

      Debian/Linux example:
      # sudo apt-get install python

   2. Install the PIP Python package manager

      Debian/Linux example:
      # sudo apt-get install pip

   3. Install required Python packages

      # pip install getopt
      # pip install textfsm
      # pip install minidom
      # pip install dicttoxml

   4. Verify the installation

      # cd <NED package>/utils/textfsm/
      # python textfsm2xml.py --help

3. Write a template
----------------------
   There are several HOWTOs on writing TextFSM templates available on the internet.

   Here are some recommended resources:
   
      The TextFSM homepage: https://github.com/google/textfsm/wiki

      Parsing CLI output with TextFSM: https://codingnetworker.com/2015/08/parse-cli-outputs-textfsm/

      OZNetNerd HOWTOs: http://www.oznetnerd.com/writing-your-own-api-part-2/
                        http://www.oznetnerd.com/writing-your-own-api-part-3/
                        
   There are also a number of ready made templates available to download:
   
      Examples from Google: https://github.com/google/textfsm/tree/master/examples
      NTC Ansible templates: https://github.com/networktocode/ntc-templates/tree/master/templates

   Follow the steps below when writing your TextFSM template:

   1. Fetch a CLI dump from the device containing the raw data to be parsed.
      This dump shall be as extensive as possible. For example data that shall be parsed as
      lists shall contain at least two entries. Save the dump to a file.

   2. Find out the path to the TextFSM python module installed on the host

      # pip show textfsm
        Name: textfsm
        Version: 0.4.1
        Summary: Python module for parsing semi-structured text into python tables.
        Home-page: https://github.com/google/textfsm
        Author: Google
        Author-email: textfsm-dev@googlegroups.com
        License: Apache License, Version 2.0
        Location: /usr/local/lib/python2.7/site-packages  <-- THIS IS THE PATH
        Requires: 
        Required-by: 

   3. Create a draft template file. It must have the extension ".textfsm". For example "foo.textfsm".

   4  Add some parser rules to the template.

   5. Verify that the rules work as expected using the TextFSM tool standalone (installed with pip above)

      # <path to textfsm>/textfsm <the template file> <the CLI dump file>

   6. Iterate steps 4 and 5 until the parser template is fully functional

   7. Verify that the parser template can be used to generate a proper XML dump

      # cd <NED package>/utils/textfsm
      # python textfsm2xml.py --template=<the template file> --file=<the CLI dump file>

   8. Store the parser template in the NED package

      # cp <the template file> <NED package>/utils/textfsm/templates


   Example using the Cisco Version parser template bundled with the TextFSM examples:

      # python /usr/local/lib/python2.7/site-packages/textfsm.py cisco_version_template.textfsm cisco_version_example
      
      FSM Template:
      Value Model (\S+) 
      Value Memory (\S+)
      Value ConfigRegister (0x\S+)
      Value Uptime (.*)
      Value Version (.*?)
      Value ReloadReason (.*)
      Value ReloadTime (.*)
      Value ImageFile ([^"]+)

      Start
        ^Cisco IOS Software.*Version ${Version},
        ^.*uptime is ${Uptime}
        ^System returned to ROM by ${ReloadReason}
        ^System restarted at ${ReloadTime}
        ^System image file is "${ImageFile}"
        ^cisco ${Model} .* with ${Memory} bytes of memory
        ^Configuration register is ${ConfigRegister}

    FSM Table:
    ['Model', 'Memory', 'ConfigRegister', 'Uptime', 'Version', 'ReloadReason', 'ReloadTime', 'ImageFile']
    ['WS-C4948-10GE', '262144K', '0x2102', '3 days, 13 hours, 53 minutes', '12.2(31)SGA1', 'reload', '05:09:09 PDT Wed Apr 2 2008', 'bootflash:cat4500-entservicesk9-mz.122-31.SGA1.bin']


    # cd <NED package>/utils/textfsm
    # python textfsm2xml.py --template=cisco_version_template.textfsm --file=cisco_version_example 
    <?xml version="1.0" ?>
    <top>
       <item>
          <uptime>3 days, 13 hours, 53 minutes</uptime>
          <config-register>0x2102</config-register>
          <reload-time>05:09:09 PDT Wed Apr 2 2008</reload-time>
          <reload-reason>reload</reload-reason>
          <version>12.2(31)SGA1</version>
          <memory>262144K</memory>
          <image-file>bootflash:cat4500-entservicesk9-mz.122-31.SGA1.bin</image-file>
          <model>WS-C4948-10GE</model>
      </item>
   </top>
   
   
4. Generate a YANG model
------------------------
  When the parser template is finished it is necessary to instrument the NED's live status YANG model
  with a matching schema snippet.

  There is a tool named XML2Yang and bundled with the NED that can be used to make this process significantly easier.
  It takes a XML dump and tries to build a matching YANG model out of it. Since this tool is using a best effort 
  approach the provided XML dump must contain enough information for the tool to make the correct assumptions:

  - All possible nodes must be available in the XML 
  - XML nodes representing lists must at least contain two entries
  - It assumes the first leaf node in the first entry of a list represents the key element. 
    If this assumption is not correct the generated YANG model needs to be corrected manually.
   
  When using the XML2Yang tool the following parameters are mandatory to provide:  
    - xml : Path to a file containing the XML dump
    - file : The file name of the parser template without path
    - command : The CLI command to be executed on the network device to make it display the corresponding CLI dump

  The following parameter is optional:
    - top : Name of the top node to be used in the YANG model. If not specified, the top name will be "item" 

  Example using the XML dump from the previous example:
   
   # cd <NED package>/utils/textfsm
   # python textfsm2xml.py --template=cisco_version_template.textfsm --file=cisco_version_example > cisco_version.xml

   In this example we choose to use "version" as the top name. 
   instead.

   # ../xml2yang/xml2yang.py --xml=cisco_version.xml --file=cisco_version_template.textfsm --command="show version" --top=version

   container version {
     tailf:meta-data "ned-livestats:parser-info" {
       tailf:meta-value "{'show':'show version','template':'cisco_version_template.textfsm'}";
     }
     config false;
     leaf uptime {
       type string;
     }
     leaf config-register {
       type string;
     }
     leaf reload-time {
       type string;
     }
     leaf reload-reason {
       type string;
     }
     leaf version {
       type string;
     }
     leaf memory {
       type string;
     }
     leaf image-file {
       type string;
     }
     leaf model {
       type string;
     }
   }

5. Extend the NED's YANG model
------------------------------
  
  1. Open the NED live status YANG file: 

  # cd  <NED package>/src
  # emacs yang/tailf-ned-<NED name>-stats.yang

  2. Add the generated YANG snippet to the YANG model

  3 Recompile the NED

  # make clean all

   
6. Reload NED package into NSO
------------------------------
  # ncs_cli -C -u admin
  # packages reload

  Test the functionality
  # show devices device <a device> live-status <path to new operational data>

  Example using the Cisco show version template:

  admin@ncs# show devices device netsim-0 live-status version 
  live-status version uptime "3 days, 13 hours, 53 minutes"
  live-status version config-register 0x2102
  live-status version reload-time "05:09:09 PDT Wed Apr 2 2008"
  live-status version reload-reason reload
  live-status version version 12.2(31)SGA1
  live-status version memory 262144K
  live-status version image-file bootflash:cat4500-entservicesk9-mz.122-31.SGA1.bin
  live-status version model WS-C4948-10GE
  admin@ncs# 

